  "review": {
    "review": "The run ended in planner exhaustion with the hero alive at full health, not an observed death. Decision 1107 (selection line 3191, accepted line 3192) retreated from 666 into 636: the recorded report calls it a non-choke endpoint, yet its only onward door, 606, was locked without a matching key. The multi-enemy selector mistook low immediate pressure for a usable retreat destination. After attacks from the pocket, decisions 1112–1113 rejected reversal along the only ordinary exit while recovery also excluded its dropped spear. Accepted waits advanced the clock; decision 1114 exhausted hungry recovery. Source inspection and isolated public-state probes reproduce the initiating retreat, without claiming recovered runtime memory. The proposed correction requires admitted onward space for non-choke retreat endpoints while preserving verified chokes and all existing safety and pickup gates. The new regression fails against the original selector and passes on the candidate; the full suite passes 2000 tests with four skips. An isolated candidate probe selects existing staff combat instead, but establishes neither combat success nor future survival.",
    "learnings": "Low approach pressure alone does not make an ordinary retreat endpoint usable. Require observed, admitted onward space unless the endpoint is a verified choke. Keep escape protection, hazard checks, mandatory collection and explicit planner exhaustion; do not substitute eating or longer waits for a blocked route."
